= Chapter 2: Integrity Rights

Integrity rights is a somewhat artificial name to group together the right to life and the right to be free from torture (or inhuman or degrading treatment). The word integrity here is used in the sense of continuity of existence, of "remaining in one-piece", as what is at stake in these rights is of course preventing unlawful killing or traumatic harm, physical, mental or moral. What is meant by physical or mental is self-explanatory. As the jurisprudence makes clear, torture does not need to be physical, it can be purely psychological (cite xxxx). What is meant by moral is somewhat novel and will be discussed further below.

What is most interesting regarding the right to life is that it is not absolute. Life can be legitimately taken away in a variety of scenarios, that are perhaps best put forward in Article 2 to the ECHR. The case of xxxxx provides an excellent example of the Court admitting that in principle, an attack that foresees and tolerates civillian death as an outcome (in times of peace) is in principle compliant with human rights, even if some violations arise in some inefficiencies in how the attack was planned. It is legitimate to take lives, even innocent lives, to save lives, if the balance is proportional and necessary.

Our present laxity with regard to the right to life may deserve some reconsideration in the light of comparative jurisprudence. In a case that closely parallels xxxxx, the German Constitutional Court strikingly opposed ever targetting a civillian, not even he is being used as a human shield as all lives are of equal value and the ammount of lives to be lost cannot be numerically compared to the amount of lives to be saved.

By contrast, the prohibition of torture however remains absolute. There is regrettably some vagueness about the details here. Courts will emphasize that torture and inhuman and degrading treatment are always prohibited, but may feel shy about characterizing as "inhuman and degrading treatment" some imposed suffering or degradation that may seem to them to effectively and proportionately pursue a legitimate aim. Likewise, there is vagueness regarding the upshot of finding some practice to be tantamount to torture or inhuman or degrading treatment: does the perpetrator need to be punished? If so, does the punishment need to have a seriousness that is conmeasurate with what nominally is the crime? If prison conditions are found to reach the threshold of torture or inhuman or degrading treatment does release need to be ordered immediately?

It is also problematic that the fierceness with which the absolute prohibition of torture is defended is not necessarily matched by social and cultural resources that would back it up. We live in a highly pragmatic era, and even within the field of human rights, we relentlessly search for, and unqualifiedly applaud "what works". But then what if torture works? What if it works better than the alternative? It is honorable to stomp one's foot and simply be unwilling to budge when it comes to this topic. Yet this is not a safe place to stop in our pragmatic age. We seem to lack and need a clear moral language to explain why torture is something we should never even consider. The laxity we have with the right to life may in fact make it harder to remain fiercely unmovable when it comes to torture. If we are already weighting lives, why should we not do it with less-than-lethal means?

The prohibition of torture or inhuman or degrading treatment has an interesting penumbra. At times court have considered that while the word "torture" is reserve for particularly odious cases of where intense suffering is intentionally caused, there are cases that fall short of torture but nonetheless trigger "inhuman or degrading treatment". In fact some conducts may only trigger "degrading treatment" and fail to clearly hit the mark of "inhuman treatment". This is not settled jurisprudence, but it is an interesting development for the reasons that will be explained below:

The idea that harm can be moral and not just mental refers to the idea that harm is a more multidimensional concept than that of "pain". There are forms of humiliation that are starting to register in case law that one cannot imagine realistically cause mental suffering. A modern example here could be putting criminal defendants in a "metal cage" if they were dangerous animals (cite xxxx), even when other means such as handcuffs were possible. I would be more concerned about the psychology of the judge or jury being tainted with prejudice, than with some sort of psychological trauma on the defendant. If anything the "normal" alternative of handcuffs is surely more physically painful than the metal cage. The point of finding this case to be a violation of the right to be free from inhuman or degrading treatment is perhaps elsewhere. It is to say that some form of "moral harm" is done to to the victim, whereby his honor or dignity is damaged, even if no physical *or mental* scars are to be found.

The idea that we can be harmed morally and not just psychological is one that should be of particular interest within the field of human rights. Some issues are seen in a distorted light if the idea of harm remains tied to physical or mental pain or disability. In this day and age it is common to see complaints that plaques honoring the names of slavers or terrorists should be taken out of public buildings. However, if harm is reduced to physical or mental pain or disability, such complaints appear to be "tempests in a teapot". Even less persuasive are attempts to frame these complaints as trying to prevent the repetition of such historical events; the law cannot concern itself with remote chains of causation. But these complaints are surely made on another register, and fit ill with the language of harm when this is strictly related only to pain or disability. Harm has another dimension, and purely moral harms (perhaps better dignitarian harm) may enter the penumbra of the prohibition of inhuman and degrading treatment.

It may be proposed that if anything, this dignitarian dimension seems to always be present in torture. A medical procedure, say done in an emergency situation where anesthesia is not available, is never torture even if extremely painful. Such procedure leaves the dignity of the person untouched. Real torture, on the other hand, embodies dignitarian harm in physical and mental suffering.


